Illustrative numbers (a hypothetical toy setup: NumPy logistic regression trained by SGD for 3 epochs, 200 training rows, a fixed 100-row validation set, 200 seeds; they are not reproduced here and will differ for your model, so measure your own):

- mean accuracy 0.762, standard deviation about 0.0135, minimum 0.72, maximum 0.79;
- the share of seeds with accuracy below 0.75 was 14% (about one in seven, matching the reported flakiness);
- below 0.74: 2%; below 0.73: 0.5%; below 0.72: 0%.

In this illustration 0.75 sits within one standard deviation of the mean and fails often, while a floor of 0.70 is never reached in 200 seeds. A workable check is: fixed seed, assert accuracy >= 0.70 as a smoke floor (this tells you that the wiring is not broken, not that the model is good), plus property tests (loss decreases; tiny batch can be overfit). If a stricter bound is wanted, note that 3 standard deviations below the mean (about 0.72 here) already equals the worst of 200 seeds, so collect more seeds or enlarge the validation set first, and re-measure when the fixture or the model changes. Real quality gating lives in the post-merge pipeline against the baseline and slices, not in the PR check.

Worked sizing with hypothetical measurements: idle 3.6 GiB, worst-case request peak 4.2 GiB, shared-memory scratch up to 0.5 GiB. Peak is about 4.7 GiB, so a 4 GiB limit is wrong by design. Options: (a) one worker process plus threads: model 1.4 + overhead 0.4 + request peak 0.6 + shm 0.5 = 2.9 GiB, so a 3.5 GiB limit (about 20% margin) is plausible; (b) keep two workers and set the limit to about 5.5 GiB. Pod spec fragment for (a):

```yaml
resources:
  requests: {memory: 3Gi}
  limits:   {memory: 3.5Gi}
volumeMounts:
  - {name: shm, mountPath: /dev/shm}
volumes:
  - name: shm
    emptyDir: {medium: Memory, sizeLimit: 512Mi}
```

The shm volume is memory-backed, so its usage is inside the 3.5 GiB. After rollout, check that restarts stop and that the container's peak memory stays below roughly 85% of the limit (the estimated 2.9 GiB peak is already about 83% of 3.5 GiB) during a load test at your largest allowed batch.

